{
64: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated reason for repeating measurements.", question: "According to the third paragraph, why are mobile survey routes repeated at similar times?", option1: "To prevent fixed sensors from recording regional weather", option2: "To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions", option3: "To ensure that every route passes through an irrigated field", option4: "To make all surface materials reach the same temperature", correctAnswer: "To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions", explanation: "Step 1: Heat differences among locations change during the day.<br><br>Step 2: A single route may also capture a cloud, traffic delay, or sprinkler, so repetition helps identify patterns that persist beyond those temporary events.<br><br>Answer: To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
65: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a methodological term.", question: "As used in the sixth paragraph, the word 'precision' most nearly refers to an appearance of:", option1: "equal temperatures across neighborhoods.", option2: "rapid movement by survey volunteers.", option3: "exact and comprehensive measurement.", option4: "agreement about which policy to select.", correctAnswer: "exact and comprehensive measurement.", explanation: "Step 1: The paragraph warns that a detailed map may contain placement errors, sampling gaps, and very local readings.<br><br>Step 2: The map can nevertheless look exact, so 'precision' refers to an appearance of exact and comprehensive measurement.<br><br>Answer: exact and comprehensive measurement.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
66: {skill_tested: "Identify the central claim of a measurement argument.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central claim?", option1: "Distributed sensors prove that official weather stations produce incorrect temperatures.", option2: "Urban heat is caused by surface color alone and can be solved by planting trees.", option3: "Neighborhood maps should determine policy without information about residents or buildings.", option4: "Regional and local measurements answer different questions and are strongest when their scales and limitations are understood.", correctAnswer: "Regional and local measurements answer different questions and are strongest when their scales and limitations are understood.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage explains the regional value of standardized stations and the local detail supplied by distributed sensors.<br><br>Step 2: It also identifies limitations of each scale and concludes that the apparent conflict disappears when their purposes are understood.<br><br>Answer: Regional and local measurements answer different questions and are strongest when their scales and limitations are understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
67: {skill_tested: "Explain the function of the opening field observation.", question: "The volunteer's walk in the first paragraph primarily serves to:", option1: "introduce variation that prompts a distinction between local and regional measurement.", option2: "demonstrate that the official station was malfunctioning that evening.", option3: "prove that irrigated fields are always the coolest places in a city.", option4: "establish the route later recommended for every heat survey.", correctAnswer: "introduce variation that prompts a distinction between local and regional measurement.", explanation: "Step 1: The sensor changes as the volunteer crosses pavement and a field, while the official station reports one city temperature.<br><br>Step 2: The passage immediately says the station answers a different question rather than calling it wrong.<br><br>Step 3: The anecdote therefore frames the central issue as measurement at different scales.<br><br>Answer: introduce variation that prompts a distinction between local and regional measurement.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
68: {skill_tested: "Infer why similar daytime peaks can produce different risks.", question: "Two neighborhoods with the same afternoon maximum may have different heat risks primarily because:", option1: "only one neighborhood is included in the official forecast.", option2: "stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", option3: "daytime sensors cannot record temperatures near masonry.", option4: "the cooler neighborhood necessarily has more air-conditioned offices.", correctAnswer: "stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", explanation: "Step 1: Masonry can release stored heat slowly after sunset, especially where vegetation is limited.<br><br>Step 2: Health risk depends on duration and opportunity for recovery, not only the highest temperature.<br><br>Step 3: Equal peaks can therefore conceal unequal overnight exposure.<br><br>Answer: stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
69: {skill_tested: "Analyze why the effect of one urban feature cannot be generalized.", question: "The fourth paragraph rejects assigning one fixed cooling value to every tree mainly because:", option1: "trees influence temperature only when planted beside official stations.", option2: "tree shade always increases nighttime heat released by masonry.", option3: "a feature's effect depends on scale and interactions with surrounding cover, buildings, moisture, and airflow.", option4: "mobile sensors cannot measure temperatures beneath vegetation.", correctAnswer: "a feature's effect depends on scale and interactions with surrounding cover, buildings, moisture, and airflow.", explanation: "Step 1: A small tree may shade one bench without cooling an intersection, and a park's reach changes with wind.<br><br>Step 2: The paragraph then directs researchers to examine combinations of environmental features.<br><br>Step 3: These examples show that cooling is contextual rather than a fixed property of any individual tree.<br><br>Answer: a feature's effect depends on scale and interactions with surrounding cover, buildings, moisture, and airflow.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
70: {skill_tested: "Determine the relationship between physical evidence and policy choice.", question: "Why does the passage state that temperature data cannot select a policy by themselves?", option1: "Sensors cannot identify any physical heat pattern accurately.", option2: "Every neighborhood must adopt the same combination of interventions.", option3: "Official stations prohibit cities from using local measurements in planning.", option4: "The appropriate response also depends on exposure, buildings, resources, and residents' circumstances.", correctAnswer: "The appropriate response also depends on exposure, buildings, resources, and residents' circumstances.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage contrasts an office block with reliable cooling and a slightly cooler residential block without it.<br><br>Step 2: It lists interventions suited to different forms of exposure.<br><br>Step 3: A physical reading identifies where heat exists, but social and building context determines what response addresses the resulting risk.<br><br>Answer: The appropriate response also depends on exposure, buildings, resources, and residents' circumstances.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
71: {skill_tested: "Synthesize safeguards needed to support a local heat conclusion.", question: "Which study design would best support a claim that one neighborhood consistently retains more nighttime heat than another?", option1: "Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", option2: "Compare one sensor from each neighborhood on a single afternoon.", option3: "Use the citywide station and assign its reading to every block in both neighborhoods.", option4: "Survey only homes whose residents already participate in environmental programs.", correctAnswer: "Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", explanation: "Step 1: Repetition at matched times reduces the influence of temporary events and changing daily temperature gaps.<br><br>Step 2: Calibration and documented placement address instrument distortion and false precision.<br><br>Step 3: Recording interacting physical features helps explain a persistent difference rather than merely mapping it.<br><br>Answer: Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
72: {skill_tested: "Reconcile seemingly conflicting measurements by identifying their scales.", question: "A neighborhood sensor reads several degrees above the official station for three evenings. Based on the passage, the most justified interpretation is that:", option1: "the official station should be discarded because regional records are useless.", option2: "the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", option3: "the neighborhood sensor proves that every home nearby has the same indoor temperature.", option4: "the neighborhood difference must be caused entirely by dark pavement.", correctAnswer: "the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", explanation: "Step 1: Official stations are standardized for regional comparison and intentionally avoid nearby distortions.<br><br>Step 2: Repeated local readings can reveal conditions created by surfaces, buildings, vegetation, and airflow.<br><br>Step 3: Neither measurement establishes every indoor condition or a single cause, but their difference is compatible once each spatial scale is identified.<br><br>Answer: the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
